2.0.0 cutover (I6, awaiting local independent review and owner approval)

- I1 through I5 are approved and merged. I5 merged in XORcyst #101
  (6095465) and Nesrev #170 (30b8382). Stage evidence and approvals are retained
  under design/v2/, including IMPLEMENTATION_HISTORY.md.
- The normal xasm/xlnk now use the shared implementation in ir/. Retired
  object bytecode, alternate engines/executables and listing-version selection
  are removed. Only XOROBJ2 objects are accepted; rebuild every object.
- Complete local review before publishing the I6 branches. Coordinate the
  Nesrev tool pin and shared tooling changes on a branch from public master.
  Never push Nesrev projects-branch history. No release tag or system install
  precedes owner approval and green CI.
- Re-pin Nesrev to the actual XORcyst merge commit before publishing its PR;
  the local review commit may change under squash/rebase. Before release or
  installation, activate the tested local projects integration together with
  the tool switch. See design/v2/I6_RELEASE_CHECKLIST.md; migration is prepared
  locally, while the live checkout remains on its current toolchain.
- Required gates: exact frozen Metroid/Zelda products and all 15 source-built
  ROMs (dpadhero2 is mandatory), Nesrev consumers/bundles, clean source archive,
  installed normal tools, tests and sanitizers. Frozen converter assets and
  project inputs stay untouched. Old dpadhero song objects are non-blocking;
  no recovery, conversion or old-format reader work is authorized.
- Report per-tool time, memory and artifact sizes. Retain the accepted I4
  small-link memory exception and I5 bounded-cost timing decision; investigate
  material regressions without disproportionate optimization for milliseconds.
  Large-project/analysis memory remains below 1.30 times the frozen baseline.

Known limitations retained from reviewed I3–I5

- Leading unnamed RAM data containing $ still fails object writing with an
  unlocated anchor error. Data after a surviving RAM label works. Any fix must
  retain checks for other initializer symbols. This is a tracked limitation,
  not an approved semantic change.
- Some inherited parser and collector allocation failures are not diagnosed:
  summary, text scope/source and xref data provenance can silently omit facts;
  index, consumers and coverage can fail without explanatory diagnostics.
  Output staging preserves files on detected errors, not ignored failures.
- Rich analysis expands shared aliases into trees and can use exponential
  memory. Ordinary values/objects use shared graphs. Text listings avoid this
  expression reconstruction. Investigate with measured project evidence.
- Diagnostic listings may be unavailable with FCEUX ROM export after a syntax
  error or unresolved storage, since the output set cannot be planned safely.
  Available diagnostic listings have provisional CPU extents and no final bytes.
- Very long runs of shared labels with sizeof repeatedly scan the leading
  labels (cubic synthetic work). The reviewed 800/1600-label cases took
  0.41/6.6 seconds; neither Metroid nor Zelda uses sizeof. Collect the run once
  if this becomes a material workload. Duplicate size diagnostics also remain.
- Unused-label warnings through constant-alias chains differ from v1, as
  recorded by the I4/I5 reviews. Other captured alias/local diagnostics are
  covered by the cutover source integration tests.
- Linker publication stages all files, then renames each independently. A
  later publication failure can leave an earlier file replaced. Assembler
  products instead use the reviewed rollback transaction.
- Retain the contract's explicit binary/link differences: late wide instruction
  range policy, data-label storage counts, and mode-specific origin/bank/import
  features. Do not silently unify them during cleanup.

Future work needs an existing-project requirement or an owner decision.
See XORCYST_V2_DESIGN.md and XORCYST_V2_CONTRACTS.md for approved semantics;
MIGRATING_TO_2.0.md describes the user-facing transition.
